---
id: org.dxos.review.non-negotiables
name: NonNegotiables
version: 0.1.0
---

Repo-wide review rules derived from the `CLAUDE.md` non-negotiables. Each `rule`
block below is discovered by the agentic-review harness, matched against the
changed files its globs select, and applied by a focused subagent. A `rule` is
just one of the block types an `.mdl` document may define; descriptor documents
(`SPEC.mdl`, `PLUGIN.mdl`) define none and are skipped.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule no-sleep-in-test: No sleep or polling in tests
  Tests must not use `sleep`, `setTimeout`/`setInterval` waits, or busy-poll
  loops to synchronize with async work — they are slow and flaky. Prefer a
  `Trigger`, `waitForCondition`, an ECHO query subscription, or Effect
  `TestClock` (which virtualizes `Effect.sleep`). A real macrotask turn is
  acceptable only when a test needs one across runtimes — say so if a flagged
  case is that exception.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.test.ts
    - packages/**/*.test.tsx
  grep: sleep|setTimeout|setInterval
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-casts: No casts to silence the type-checker
  Casts that suppress the type system hide the real error. Flag `as any`,
  `as unknown as T` (the double-cast escape hatch), widened `any` in signatures,
  and the non-null assertion `!` (as in `foo!.bar` or `arr![0]`). Fix the type
  at its source. `as const` is allowed — do not flag it, nor a `!` inside a
  string literal or comment.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: as any|as unknown|: any\b|<any\b|[^!=]![.\[]
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule no-compat-shims: No compatibility re-exports when moving code
  When code moves, every call site must be updated in the same change — do not
  leave a compatibility re-export or shim behind at the old location. Flag
  re-export shims kept only for backwards compatibility (e.g. `export * from
  './new-location'` where the implementation used to live) and comments
  announcing a temporary compat layer. Genuine barrel/`index.ts` public-API
  re-exports are expected — do not flag those.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: compat|backward|@deprecated|re-export|export \*
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule no-echo-internal-in-sdk: SDK and app code uses the public ECHO API
  `@dxos/echo/internal` is ECHO's private surface and carries no compatibility
  guarantee, so sdk, plugin, and app code must import from the public entry
  (`@dxos/echo`) whenever an equivalent exists — e.g. `Annotation.make` in place
  of `createAnnotationHelper`, `Obj`/`Ref`/`Type` in place of their internal
  counterparts. Flag an `@dxos/echo/internal` import and name the public
  alternative. Do not flag an import with no public equivalent; say so instead.
  Packages under `packages/core/echo/**` are ECHO itself — never flag those.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/sdk/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/**/*.tsx
    - packages/plugins/**/*.ts
    - packages/plugins/**/*.tsx
    - packages/apps/**/*.ts
    - packages/apps/**/*.tsx
  grep: '@dxos/echo/internal'
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule private-new-packages: New packages must be private
  Every newly-added package must set `"private": true` in its `package.json`;
  the flag is removed manually only once a trusted publisher exists. Flag a
  `package.json` that is newly added in this change and lacks `"private": true`.
  Do not flag existing published packages that are merely modified. If you
  cannot tell whether the package is new, note the uncertainty rather than
  asserting a violation.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/package.json
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule workspace-deps: In-repo deps use the workspace protocol
  Any in-repo `@dxos/*` dependency must use the workspace protocol, never a
  version range or the catalog. Flag an `@dxos/*` entry in
  `dependencies`/`devDependencies` that is not `workspace:*`. In
  `peerDependencies` it must be `workspace:^` (caret) — a `workspace:*` peer
  reads as out-of-range on any bump and cascades a spurious major, so flag a
  `*` peer too. Ignore `@dxos/*` names external to this monorepo.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/package.json
  grep: @dxos/
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule bounded-live-state: Every collection of live entities has an explicit upper bound
  State that grows with use must have a hard cap, enforced where the entity is
  admitted: a maximum number of processes a manager may have live, of objects a
  database or space accepts, of entries a registry, index, queue or cache holds.
  A collection that only ever grows is a slow-motion outage — an Automerge
  document or a persisted map has no size alarm, a query over it gets slower with
  every entry, and a client that has accumulated a year of history hits the
  wall in a way no test ever did. The cap is a named constant with a doc
  comment saying what it protects, and exceeding it is a typed error the caller
  sees (a domain error, a rejected operation), never silent truncation.
  `MAX_OBJECT_DIAGNOSTICS` in `packages/core/echo/echo-client/src/hypergraph.ts`
  and `MAX_TRACKED_INVOCATIONS` in
  `packages/core/compute/compute-runtime/src/triggers/trigger-dispatcher.ts`
  are the canonical shape.
  Flag: a `register`/`add`/`spawn`/`put`/`insert`/`push` path that appends to a
  persisted or long-lived map, array, table or queue with no check against a
  maximum before the write; a cap that exists but is unnamed (a bare number
  inline) or undocumented; and a cap enforced only on read (a `slice`/`limit`
  on the list side) while the write side stays unbounded. Report it on the line
  of the write, and name the collection and what grows it.
  Do not flag: a collection whose membership is already bounded by construction
  (one entry per space, one per configured plugin, a fixed enum of keys); one
  whose entries are removed on the same path that adds them so its size is a
  function of concurrency, not history; a primitive that documents its own
  bound; user-authored ECHO content, whose size is the user's to decide; or
  in-memory state scoped to a single call.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: \.set\(|\.push\(|\.add\(|db\.add\(|INSERT INTO
  severity: error
```

```mdl
rule collect-dead-entities: Terminated entries are retained up to a cap and then collected
  Keeping a record of an entity that has finished — a terminated process, a
  completed invocation, a settled request, a closed connection — is legitimate:
  a list of processes should show what ran and how it ended. But the retention
  must be bounded and automatic. There is a named cap on how many dead entries
  (or how old, when age is the natural measure) the collection keeps, and
  crossing it evicts the oldest on the same path that adds the new one, or from
  a timer or sweep that already runs for the collection's owner. Collection
  never depends on a client remembering to delete, on an operator running a
  script, or on a future "cleanup task" — the entries outlive all three. The
  `invocations` and `errors` rings in
  `packages/core/compute/compute-runtime/src/triggers/trigger-dispatcher.ts`
  (`[...invocations, invocation].slice(-MAX_TRACKED_INVOCATIONS)`) are the
  canonical shape: the eviction is one expression on the write path.
  Flag: a status field, `completedAt`, `terminated`, `done` or equivalent whose
  entries are written into a persisted or long-lived collection but never
  removed by any code path; removal that exists only as an explicit
  `delete`/`unregister`/`remove` API with no automatic trigger; a cap on live
  entries (see `bounded-live-state`) that counts dead entries against it, so a
  manager that has finished many processes can no longer start one; and a
  retention constant with no comment saying why that number. Report it on the
  line that transitions the entry to its terminal state, or the write that
  persists it there, and say what accumulates.
  Do not flag: entries removed synchronously on termination (nothing dead is
  retained, so there is nothing to collect); a store whose backend enforces
  expiry natively, or rows an existing scheduled sweep already prunes — cite
  the sweep; or an audit log that is deliberately append-only and lives in a
  store sized for it, when the file says so.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/src/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/src/**/*.tsx
  grep: completedAt|terminated|Terminated|status: '(done|completed|failed)'|_tag: '(Done|Completed|Failed)'
  severity: error
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent to changed files.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:      Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:      Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:  warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:     dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
```
